<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khujand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Leninabad, Khodjent</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khujand is the second city of Tajikistan, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>. One of the oldest towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, it is identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 329 BCE, and was a station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. Known as Leninabad from 1936 to 1991, its region supplied much of the Soviet-era Tajik ruling elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khujand.jpg" alt="Khujand" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EH · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_to_Khujand.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Khujand, also spelled Khodjent, is the second-largest city of Tajikistan and the capital of its northernmost province, Sughd. It stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> river at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a> and was a major city on the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. It is one of the oldest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, dating back about 2,500 years to the Persian Empire. Its fortress, built over 2,500 years ago, has been destroyed and renovated several times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Khujand may have been the site of Cyropolis, said to have been founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> on his last expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a> shortly before his death. In 329 BCE Alexander the Great built his furthest Greek settlement near Cyropolis and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>, Alexandria the Furthest. It served as a bastion for Greek settlers against the nomadic Scythian tribes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. According to the Roman writer Curtius, it kept its Hellenistic culture as late as 30 BCE.</p><p>In the early eighth century the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> captured the city, and a process of Islamicization began. It passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> in the late ninth century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a> in 999. The Karakhitans conquered it in 1137, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshahs</a> in 1211. In 1220 it strongly resisted the Mongol hordes and was laid waste. It was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> in the 14th century and then of the Timurid dynasty, under which it flourished greatly. The Shaybanid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> annexed it, and the Kokand Khanate took it in 1802. Bukhara regained it in 1842 and lost it a few decades later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>In 1866 the city became part of the Russian General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of Turkestan. Threats of forced conscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> led to protests in July 1916 that turned violent when demonstrators attacked Russian soldiers. In 1929 the Soviets transferred Khujand and the surrounding area, inhabited mainly by ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>, from the Uzbek SSR to the new Tajik SSR. The city was renamed Leninabad on 10 January 1936 and regained its original name in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the people of Khujand are ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> is the main religion. The Historical Museum of Sughd is housed inside the Khujand Fortress, and the Sheikh Muslihiddin mausoleum stands on the main square opposite the Panjshanbe Market, one of the largest covered markets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The city also has monuments of the 16th and 17th centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khujand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1137 he defeated the Western Karakhanids near <b>Khujand</b> and annexed Fergana and Tashkent, his army swollen by 10,000 Khitans formerly subject to the Karakhanids, and went on to take Kashgar, Khotan and Beshbalik.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1929 the republic was separated from Uzbekistan and raised to a union republic, the Tajik SSR, with the <b>Khujand</b> region added.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajik-assr">Tajik ASSR</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1929 the Tajik ASSR was separated from the Uzbek SSR and upgraded to a union republic, the Tajik SSR, with the addition of the <b>Khujand</b> district.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajik-ssr">Tajik SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>Tension between the old communist elite, largely from <b>Khujand</b> and Kulob, and an opposition of democrats, Islamists and Pamiris from Gharm and Gorno-Badakhshan erupted into civil war in 1992.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></li></ul></section>
